From: weewx-rtldavis (WeatheredScientist fork)
Subject: rtldavis: drop a second decode of one RF burst (burst gate)

ROADMAP P1.9 step 2 / #459, DEC-0223.  The demodulator works in 512-sample
blocks and finds a burst's preamble at about ten neighboring sample offsets.
When that run of offsets crosses a block boundary, the burst is decoded again
from the next block, and offsets at the ends of the run can carry bit errors,
inside one block too.  A corrupted copy differs from the original, so the
byte-equality duplicate filter (rtldavis-dupgate.patch) passes it, and about 1
in 65,536 also passes CRC and reaches the driver as a plausible wrong reading
(DEC-0033, DEC-0035).  #458's counters put the corrupted-copy population at
about 9,700 a day on a live station.

The gate keys on each frame's position in the sample stream (blocks read x 512
+ the frame's offset in the buffer): the receiver's own count, which the frame
cannot corrupt and which survives a retune.  A copy sits 1 to 13 samples from
the original; the next transmission from the same ISS is at least 2.5625 s
(688,800 samples) away.  The window is one packet's airtime, preamble plus
packet (1,344 samples, 5 ms), read from the packet config at startup:

  * a frame inside the window of the last ACCEPTED frame is a copy of a burst
    already accepted and hopped on: dropped, no hop, `burst copy:` logged;
  * two CRC-valid frames inside the window in one block are distinct decodes
    of one burst (Parse already removed identical ones): neither can be
    trusted, both dropped, `burst conflict:` logged.

The duplicate filter runs first and is unchanged; its log line gains the gap
in samples so the window can be checked against real copies.  Only an
accepted frame moves the reference.  Prior art for a content-agnostic gate:
lheijst/rtldavis#2 (LloydR), which keys a 2 s wall-clock window on the frame's
own transmitter id.

tests/test_burstgate_patch.py audits every line this patch adds to or removes
from main.go, runs the gate's Go tests, and replays synthetic bursts through
the real Demodulate and Parse.

Applies AFTER rtldavis-dupgate.patch and rtldavis-crcstats.patch, to the
rtldavis source bundled in src.tgz.

--- a/burstgate/burstgate.go
+++ b/burstgate/burstgate.go
@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+package burstgate
+
+// Modified by the weewx-rtldavis project (WeatheredScientist): the burst gate,
+// ROADMAP P1.9 step 2 / #459, DEC-0223.
+//
+// The demodulator works in 512-sample blocks and finds a burst's preamble at
+// about ten neighboring sample offsets. When that run of offsets crosses a block
+// boundary, the burst is decoded again from the next block; offsets at the ends
+// of the run sample near symbol edges and can carry bit errors, inside one block
+// too. A corrupted copy differs from the original, so the byte-equality
+// duplicate filter (DEC-0135) passes it, and about 1 in 65,536 also passes CRC
+// (DEC-0033, DEC-0035).
+//
+// The gate keys on the frame's position in the sample stream -- the receiver's
+// own count, which the frame cannot corrupt and which survives a retune. A copy
+// sits 1 to 13 samples from the original; the next real transmission from the
+// same ISS is at least 2.5625 s (688,800 samples) away. A frame that starts
+// within one packet's airtime of the last accepted frame is the same burst.
+//
+// The gate decides nothing on its own: main.go asks Copy and Conflicts and
+// drops the frame. Only Accept moves the reference, so a chain of dropped copies
+// cannot ratchet the window forward.
+
+// Pos is a frame's position in the demodulator's sample stream: the number of
+// blocks read times the block size, plus the frame's offset in the buffer. The
+// buffer's origin trails the newest block by a constant, so differences between
+// positions are exact sample counts.
+func Pos(block int64, blockSize, idx int) int64 {
+	return block*int64(blockSize) + int64(idx)
+}
+
+type Gate struct {
+	window int64 // samples; a frame closer than this to the last accepted one is the same burst
+	last   int64 // position of the last ACCEPTED frame
+	have   bool  // false until the first accept
+}
+
+func New(window int64) *Gate {
+	return &Gate{window: window}
+}
+
+func (g *Gate) Window() int64 {
+	return g.window
+}
+
+// Gap returns the distance in samples from pos to the last accepted frame, or
+// -1 before the first accept.
+func (g *Gate) Gap(pos int64) int64 {
+	if !g.have {
+		return -1
+	}
+	if pos < g.last {
+		return g.last - pos
+	}
+	return pos - g.last
+}
+
+// Copy reports whether a frame at pos lies inside the window of the last
+// accepted frame: a second decode of a burst that was already accepted.
+func (g *Gate) Copy(pos int64) bool {
+	d := g.Gap(pos)
+	return d >= 0 && d < g.window
+}
+
+// Accept records an accepted frame at pos.
+func (g *Gate) Accept(pos int64) {
+	g.last = pos
+	g.have = true
+}
+
+// Conflicts takes the positions of the CRC-valid frames from one Parse call.
+// Parse has already removed byte-identical frames, so two of them inside the
+// window are distinct decodes of one burst and neither can be trusted. For each
+// frame it returns the distance to its nearest such neighbor, or -1 when there
+// is none.
+func (g *Gate) Conflicts(pos []int64) []int64 {
+	out := make([]int64, len(pos))
+	for i := range pos {
+		out[i] = -1
+		for j := range pos {
+			if i == j {
+				continue
+			}
+			d := pos[i] - pos[j]
+			if d < 0 {
+				d = -d
+			}
+			if d < g.window && (out[i] < 0 || d < out[i]) {
+				out[i] = d
+			}
+		}
+	}
+	return out
+}
